> My own thoughts are:
> * unless you're a gray dragon, you _have_ to wish for the EotW, PYEC, EotA
>   (neutral) or OoDet, SoMight or MMoMerlin (lawful), and they can't be
>   your quest artifact as whatever can steal it can touch you the very next
>   round.

There's only one item grabber with touch of death whom you _will_
encounter, and he's susceptible to death rays and disintegration
blasts (the latter being my method of choice when playing a black
dragon).

>  Somehow, SoMight works when carried while Magicbane doesn't.

Magicbane is no quest artefact...

> * for anything with a bite attack (ie, most viable mons), you'll want to
>   genocide c and N (not strictly mandatory, but...)

Why N? They have no defensive effects which get triggered by physical
contact.

You must be careful with all physical attacks which aren't covered by
armour: butt, bite, sting, tentacle, kicking when not wearing boots,
hugging when not wearing shirt/armour/cloak...
At least if you're not petrification resistant.

> * regeneration is mandatory (you regenerate only 1hp/20turns when polyed)

And boost your hitpoints as far as you can after the polymorph; use
unchanging to make sure you only poly once - any extra hitpoints
gained in your polymorphed shape (potions of extra healing, level
gains etc.) will be lost when you transform back to your normal shape.
You _can_ get around the low regeneration by healing spells, potion
use or a life-draining artefact.

> * a ring/amulet eating spree before the final poly is close to a must --
>   or a must for non-pacifist purple worms

I think purple worms aren't a particularly good monster to poly into;
they're effectively paralysed for a _long_ time everytime they swallow
something.

> * if anything important falls into a pit, you lost if you can fly (there
>   is no way to intentionally descend into one)

As far as i can tell, in 3.4.1 you _can_ pick up items from pits while
flying. While levitating, you can pick up lightweight items by
applying a bullwhip - requires being able to wield one, of course.

> Back to ordinary humanoid monsters:
> * ascending a xorn is FUN.  You can use everything but shirts, body armour
>   and cloaks (-> no magic cancellation),

Every creature that's able to wear a hat can have 2 points of magic
cancellation. It's not particularly helpful against life drainers, but
at least it makes electric eels a bit less annoying.

>   but if you have free action,
>   walking through walls more than makes up for that restriction.

Oh yes, xorns are very nice. Being able to make shortcuts through
Gehennom was a lot of fun; and there's always a quick escape
available.

The most fun i had with the pit fiend, though (although the water
nymph was pretty crazy, too - able to swim and generally won fights by
taking everything dangerous out of monsters' hands): that had been a
tourist who gave in to the Dark Side - converted to chaotic after
finishing the quest - and proceeded to flatten everything with
Stormbringer. You get two weapon attacks per round, _and_ you can
double-wield, giving you four blows per melee round. The downsides:
pit fiends are fairly slow, can't wear anything on their bodies, have
a hug attack (necessitating a genocide of "c" or _extreme_
carefulness) and obviously have no reflection while #twoweaponing, as
the amulet slot will be taken for unchanging.
The dual Stormbringer attack made the char so powerful at handling
drainable foes that i deliberately fooled around with two of the
riders, producing kill counts of:
Pestilence (40 times)
Famine (30 times)

> * arch-liches are fun, but nowhere close to xorns.  Their carrying
>   capacity is halved compared to humans, and being undead means !pray
>   (unless you're chaotic -- I wasn't).  Too bad, your extra attack is cold
>   instead of death...

I think the spell attacks are coded specifically as effects which
_monsters_ can use on the _player_. In all other cases, they just
don't happen. And if you're not chaotic, you can convert or wear a
helm of opposite alignment ...

> * vampire lords have great natural AC, fly and have a bite drain level
>   attack (hard to believe, but I really _did_ unintentionally hit a
>   cockatrice after polying but before switching my AoLS to the AoUC. 

Biting a cockatrice is _traditional_, nothing wrong with that,
particularly when you survive. <g>

Interesting challenging choices for shapes to 'morph into include: 
cockatrice - incredible defensive powers, but _slow_ and _weak_
gelatinous cube, fire elemental, air elemental, fire and energy vortex
- good defensive effects, some can fly, gelatinous cubes can eat
various strange items; downside is they're all blind and can't
usefully interact with the dungeon (can't open or kick down doors
e.g.)
quantum mechanic - you won't kill many monsters in melee <g>
Umber Hulk - customise your dungeon
any "S" monster - the ultra challenge, because you can't pick up
anything

